Good, use opaque_fragment.

Rim injection:
```
shader.vertexShader = shader.vertexShader
  .replace('#include <common>', '#include <common>\nvarying vec3 vRimNormal;\nvarying vec3 vRimView;')
  .replace('#include <project_vertex>', `#include <project_vertex>
    vRimNormal = normalize(mat3(modelMatrix) * objectNormal);
    vec4 rwp = modelMatrix * vec4(transformed, 1.0);
    vRimView = normalize(cameraPosition - rwp.xyz);`);
shader.fragmentShader = shader.fragmentShader
  .replace('#include <common>', '#include <common>\nvarying vec3 vRimNormal;\nvarying vec3 vRimView;\nuniform vec3 uRimColor;\nuniform float uRimPower;\nuniform float uRimStrength;')
  .replace('#include <opaque_fragment>', `
    float rimF = pow(1.0 - clamp(dot(normalize(vRimNormal), normalize(vRimView)), 0.0, 1.0), uRimPower);
    outgoingLight += uRimColor * rimF * uRimStrength;
    #include <opaque_fragment>`);
```
Actually normalize(vRimNormal) in world; good enough (non-uniform scale slight issue).
Uniforms in onBeforeCompile need added to shader.uniforms; material.userData for updates maybe.

Need customProgramCacheKey based on rim values? Uniforms are per-material; shader program from same key. Default cache key includes material type. Setting `.customProgramCacheKey = () => 'toon-rim'` to share programs but uniforms separate per material clone. Good.

`toon(color, opts={})`:
```
const m = new THREE.MeshToonMaterial({ color, gradientMap: GRADIENT_MAP, ...opts });
m.onBeforeCompile = ...
